## 1. Introduction
MicroRNA (miRNA), a short and non-coding RNA sequence, plays a vital regulation role in cell by adjusting the protein coding genes (He et al., 2004). The aberrant adjustment of miRNA, usually, are associated with some diseases such as kidney disease, central nervous system relevant diseases, cardiovascular disease and even fatal cancers (Londin et al., 2015; Hammond, 2006). Considering the important roles of miRNA in biological processes and its low-abundance in biological samples, many methods based on the enzyme-mediated isothermal amplification strategy have been proposed, including loop-mediated isothermal amplification (Tomita et al., 2008), polymerase chain reac­ tion (PCR) (Zhao et al., 2015; Raymond et al., 2005), single primer isothermal amplification (Kurn et al., 2005), rolling circle amplification (RCA) (Zhang et al., 2014b; Zhao et al., 2008), and so on. However, the system adopting enzymes is susceptible to the sophisticated biological environment, and therefore brings out some undesired interference of the biomolecular amplification and recognition processes. Hence, it is meaningful to construct the enzyme-free amplification strategies for sensitive, accurate and simple quantification of miRNA in earlier diag­ nosis and treatment.
To overcome above challenges, the enzyme-free dynamic DNA selfassembly (DDSA) amplification technique such as hybridization chain reaction (HCR) (Dirks et al., 2004; Cheglakov et al., 2015; Zheng et al., 2013) and catalyzed hairpin assembly (CHA) (Liu et al., 2018; Kar­ unanayake Mudiyanselage et al., 2018; Qing et al., 2014) have been developed and considered as an appealing amplification strategy for constructing various biosensor. Instead of a recycling cleavage reaction, a hybridization process is only involved in the amplification reaction, otherwise, the efficient signal amplification process takes place at a constant temperature without involving any protein enzyme (Zhu et al., 2019). In terms of the remarkable features of HCR, these initiator-triggered cascade products of DNA are highly ordered double helices, which guarantees the signaling molecules to attach on these helices at precise and controllable density (Wilner et al., 2012). So, the final amplification signal will be improved efficiently. For the typical CHA method, the hybridization reaction of two elaborately designed hairpin probes can be cycled by initiator to output an amplified signal (Meng et al., 2020; Liu et al., 2016a). The merit of CHA is that, at a constant temperature, the catalyzed hybridization of hairpins could generate hundred-folds amplification signal with a low background and without consuming the target (Li et al., 2013). Hence, to further improve the analytical performance of biosensors for target analyte, the target-activated CHA- and HCR-based dual-cascaded isothermal ampli­ fication strategy has been developed for detecting the small sizes and low abundances of miRNA in biological samples (Zhao et al., 2021; Fu et al., 2021).
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with large surface area, wellordered porosity structure, and exposed active sites are composed of metal ions/clusters and organic linkers (Zhao et al., 2014; Liu et al., 2020). As a new class of porous crystalline materials, MOFs have attracted tremendous attentions and been applied in catalysis, separa­ tion, gas storage and analytical sensors (Li et al., 2010; Taylor-Pashow et al., 2009; Wang et al., 2011; Ling et al., 2015). In particular, Cu-MOFs, benefiting from the redox activity of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ and excellent catalytic ac­ tivity, have been used as the electroactive materials to construct elec­ trochemical sensors (Liu et al., 2016b; Shultz et al., 2011). Compared with other electrochemical sensors modified with signal molecules, the analytical performance of Cu-MOFs-based sensors can be improved by the numerous $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ to produce high electrochemical signals and the large surface area to immobilize abundant recognition molecules (Zhou et al., 2018). Besides, to further improve the conductivity and biocom­ patibility of Cu-MOFs, the monometallic or bimetallic nanoparticles modified Cu-MOFs are also explored in biosensing application. (Chen et al., 2019; Zhang et al., 2014a).
Although, a number of MOFs-based or DDSA-assisted amplification strategies have been explored to construct electrochemical biosensors for various biomarkers, the combination of DDSA and MOFs to syner­ gistically improve the detection sensitivity is rarely reported (Chai et al., 2021; Cao et al., 2021). Inspired by aforementioned facts, it is the first time that a label-free and enzyme-free electrochemical biosensor was constructed based on the CHA-HCR-Cu-MOFs amplification strategy for the highly sensitive detection of miRNA. Here, the electrochemical sig­ nals produced by $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ from Cu-MOFs, avoiding labeling the signal molecules. Furthermore, after decorating Cu-MOFs with AuNPs, the formed AuNPs/Cu-MOFs not only increased specific surface area to anchor a large number of hairpin DNA 3 (H3), but also enhanced the conductivity and biocompatibility of Cu-MOFs (Ge et al., 2013). The detection principle consists of twice amplification of input signals and the final enhancement of output signals. The twice stage amplification, on the one hand, is realized by the target-induced CHA cycles to produce plenty of dsDNA products (H1/H2). On the other hand, is realized by the HCR process between AuNPs/Cu-MOFs labeled H3 (H3-AuNPs/Cu-­ MOFs) and H4. After twice amplification processes, plenty of Cu-MOFs are immobilized on electrode surface, enhancing the output electro­ chemical signals of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ . Finally, benefiting from the CHA-HCR isothermal amplification strategy and integrating the Cu-based metal-­ organic frameworks into the HCR process, the sensitivity and selectivity of the biosensor are significantly improved for detection low-abundance target miRNA, which shows a wide linear range from 0.1 fM to $100\mathrm{pM}$ and a lower detection limit of 0.02 fM for miR-21.
