## 3. Result and discussion
## 3.1. Principle of the proposed sensing system
As schematically shown in Scheme 1, the principle of the proposed CHA-HCR-Cu-MOFs sensing strategy is composed of two parts (A and B). Scheme 1A exhibits the preparation procedure of H3-AuNPs/Cu-MOFs probes, which includes three steps. The first step is to synthesize the Cu-MOFs through hydrothermal reaction; the second step is to generate AuNPs/Cu-MOFs by in-situ reduction of $\mathrm{\HAuCl_{4}}$ on the surface of CuMOFs; finally, H3-AuNPs/Cu-MOFs is assembled via the strong inter­ action between AuNPs and $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups of $\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{H}3$ . And Scheme 1B demonstrates the main sensing process, including the CHA cycles and Cu-MOFs-mediated HCR amplification process. All of the DNA hairpins associated with the sensing process are metastable which can be only stimulated by their corresponding trigger sequence to initiate the sub­ sequent hybridization reaction and self-assemble process. When the target miR-21 is presented in the sensing system, H1 can be opened by the toehold-mediated branch migrated reaction, rendering the toehold of H2 is exposed. Subsequently, H2 is opened and hybridizes with H1 to form miR-21/H1/H2 complex, and then miR-21 is released by the stand displacement reaction. After one cycle of the CHA reaction, dsDNA (H1/ H2) formed and the released miR-21 could trigger the CHA reaction in succession to generate more H1/H2 complexes. The dsDNA products (H1/H2) of the upstream CHA cycles is a vital component in the elec­ trochemical biosensor. Because not only can it relate to the amount of the input analyte but also motivate the downstream Cu-MOFs-mediated HCR of H3-AuNPs/Cu-MOFs and H4 on the electrode surface. Thus, when CHA reaction products are added onto the MCH/P1/GE surface, lots of H1/H2 complexes can be anchored on the electrode surface by hybridizing with P1. When H3-AuNPs/Cu-MOFs and H4 are incubated with the H1/H2/MCH/P1/GE, the HCR takes place and plenty of CuMOFs are immobilized on the electrode surface, producing the signifi­ cant electrochemical signals.
## 3.2. Characterization of the synthesized nanomaterials
Then, SEM-energy-dispersive X-ray elemental mapping (SEM-EDX), X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) and zeta-potential measure­ ment were used to characterize the H3-AuNPs/Cu-MOFs. SEM-EDX re­ sults of corresponding elemental mapping reveal that C, N, O, P, Au and Cu elements existed and distributed in H3-AuNPs/Cu-MOFs uniformly (Fig. S2). The XPS characterization results of H3-AuNPs/Cu-MOFs are shown in Fig. 3, the characteristic peaks for C 1s, N 1s, O 1s, P 2p, Au 4f and Cu 2p were remarkably observed in survey XPS spectrum (Fig. 3A).
Among the rest, the fitted XPS spectra of C 1s illustrated three surface components, namely, the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC benzoic ring $(284.6~\mathrm{eV})$ , the C–O $(285.8\ \mathrm{eV})$ and the carboxylate $0{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{=}}0$ $(288.3\ \mathrm{eV})$ group of $\mathrm{{NH}_{2}}$ -BDC (Fig. 3B) (Sun et al., 2018; Liang et al., 2015; Chandra et al., 2010). And the XPS peaks of N 1s, O 1s and P 2p located at 399.4, 531.5 and 134.2 eV, respectively (Fig. 3C–E) (Shao et al., 2018; Wang et al., 2017). In Fig. 3F, the XPS doublet peaks around 84.2 and $87.9\mathrm{eV}$ were assigned to the $4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ and $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ of Au 4f, respectively (Park et al., 2015; Mahmoud et al., 2011). In Fig. 3G, the peaks at 934.4 and $954.0\mathrm{eV}$ were ascribed to Cu $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ and Cu $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ of CuO (Yao et al., 2015), and the peaks located at 943.8 and $962.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ belonged to the open $3~\mathrm{d}^{9}$ shell of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ (Talin et al., 2014; Anandan et al., 2012), which illustrated that there are no peaks ascribed to zerovalent and monovalent Cu. That is to say, the Cu-MOFs were formed by divalent Cu ions and organic ligands. Mean­ while, zeta-potential measurements were employed to attest the suc­ cessful preparation of H3-AuNPs/Cu-MOFs. It can be seen from Fig. 3H that the average potential value of Cu-MOFs was $+9.20~\mathrm{mV}$ , meaning that some $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ was exposed on the surface of Cu-MOFs (Wang et al., 2017). When AuNPs was assembled on the $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOFs surface, the zeta potential value significantly inverted to $-16.53\mathrm{mV}$ . The change of zeta potential from $+9.20\mathrm{mV}$ to $-16.53\mathrm{mV}$ was attributed to the successful decoration of positively charged Cu-MOFs with negatively charged AuNPs. After grafting $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -hairpin DNA 3 $(\mathrm{NH_{2}}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}3)$ onto the AuNPs/Cu-MOFs surface through $\mathrm{\Au{-NH}}_{2}$ covalent bonds, the zeta po­ tential value changed more negatively $(-30.23~\mathrm{mV})$ , which indicated that H3 was successfully anchored on the AuNPs/Cu-MOFs surface. The
## 3.3. Feasibility study
In order to preliminarily prove the feasibility of our sensing strategy, polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (PAGE) experiments were imple­ mented and the results were demonstrated in Fig. 4A. The bands from lane A to lane D presented target miR-21, P1, H1 and H2, respectively. The mixture of H1 and H2 showed two bands related to the hairpin probe of H1 and H2 (lane E), respectively, indicating that the hybridization between H1 and H2 didn’t occur without miR-21. In the presence of target miR-21, a new band with slow electrophoretic mobility and high molecule weight appeared in lane F, suggesting the addition of miR-21 triggered the CHA process of H1 and H2, and formed the dsDNA (H1/H2). The mixture of H1, H2 and P1 showed two bands (lane G), which represented the high molecule weight P1/H1 hybrids and H2, respectively. While a more slowly migrated band than H1/H2 and P1/H1 was observed in lane H after adding miR-21, which illus­ trated that the CHA products H1/H2 hybridized with P1 and produced the P1/H1/H2 hybrids. When both reaction systems of lane G and lane H was mixed with H3 and H4, a serious of bands with high molecule weight appeared in lane M, accompanied by the disappearance of the bands related to P1, H1, H2, H3 and H4 alone. In contrast, lane L only showed the electrophoretic bands of H2, H3, H4 and P1/H1 hybrids. These results confirmed that the CHA and HCR could proceed smoothly with the designed DNA probes in the presence of miR-21. Then, the cyclic voltammetry (CV) and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) were carried out to investigate the fabrication steps of the elec­ trochemical biosensor. With the successive assembly process of the electrochemical biosensor, the increased peak-to-peak separation on CV curves and the increased $\mathrm{R_{et}}$ of EIS indicate that the electrochemical biosensor was assembled successfully (Fig. S4 and S5).
To further investigate the feasibility of the EC biosensor, differential pulse voltammetry (DPV) measurements were conducted. Fig. 4B displays the DPV curves of different modified electrodes. It can be seen that there is no current response of MCH/P1/GE (curve a). When MCH/ P1/GE was incubated with the CHA reaction products and followed by incubation with H3-AuNPs/Cu-MOFs and H4, a strong DPV current peak of $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOFs was observed at about $-0.22\mathrm{V}$ (curve b). It was due to the fact that, in the presence of target miR-21, the cascade of CHA and HCR process was successfully executed, thus a large number of Cu-MOFs were immobilized on the GE surface and generated a strong DPV signal. Moreover, the control experiments were carried out via only lacking H4 or target miR-21 in the sensing system. When H4 was not incubated together with H3-AuNPs/Cu-MOFs on GE surface, a small current response of Cu-MOFs was observed (Fig. 4B, curve c), which was ascribed to the fact that the HCR amplification process was unable to proceed without H4, and only a few H3-AuNPs/Cu-MOFs could be immobilized on the GE surface by hybridization with H1/H2. From Fig. 4B curve d, when target miR-21 was absent in the reaction mixture, a negligible current response appeared due to the nonspecific adsorption of few Cu-MOFs. Therefore, the PAGE and DPV experimental results convincingly confirm the feasibility of the designed EC biosensor.
## 3.4. Analytical performance
For the sake of achieving the optimal sensing performance, several critical experimental parameters including the reaction temperature of
CHA, the concentration of P1 and the reaction time of HCR were sys­ tematically studied. Based on the relationship between the DPV signals of Cu-MOFs and different experimental conditions, the optimal experi­ ment condition was selected (Fig. S6). Under the optimal experimental conditions, the analytical performance of this EC biosensor was tested with DPV method. The DPV curves and the plot of peak current versus miR-21 concentration are shown in Fig. 5A and B respectively. It can be seen that the DPV peak current increased with increasing the concen­ tration of miR-21 from 0 to $1\mathrm{nM}$ . A good linear relationship between the peak current and the logarithm of miR-21 concentration was observed ranging from 0.1 fM to $100\mathrm{pM}$ (Fig. 5C). The linear regression equation is $\operatorname{I}\left(\upmu\mathrm{A}\right)=0.1123\log{\operatorname{C}_{\mathrm{miR}-21}}+0.9701$ $(\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.9936)$ with the LOD as low as 0.02 fM $(\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{N}=3)$ . Although many biosensors have been reported for detection of target nucleic acids, for example, Zhou et al. and Zhu et al. have constructed the EC and ECL biosensor for sensitive detection of miRNA. The performance of our biosensor is comparable to them or better than other biosensors for miRNA or DNA detection (Table S2). Above experiment results indicate that the sensing strategy based on CHA-HCR dual amplification technology integrated with Cu-based metal-organic frameworks has an excellent analytical performance for target miRNA.
## 3.5. Stability, reproducibility, selectivity and practical application of the EC biosensor
The stability was evaluated by testing DPV response of the EC biosensor stored at $4~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ from 1 to 13 days. The DPV response merely decreased by $3.45\%$ from the first day to the ninth day, and had a $5.73\%$ decline after 13 days storage (Fig. 5D). In addition, five electrodes parallel measurements of miR-21 at different concentration (0.5 and 50 pM) were performed to investigate the reproducibility of this EC biosensor (Fig. 5E). The relative standard deviations of two concentra­ tions were $1.34\%$ and $1.30\%$ , respectively. Selectivity is an important parameter to evaluate the performance of a sensor. Therefore, we evaluated the selectivity by comparing DPV response of the biosensor toward miR-21 with other miRNAs associated with diseases such as miR155, miR-133a, miR-134 and miR-128 at the same experiment condi­ tions, except for that the concentration of each type of miRNAs is 10 times higher than miR-21. As shown in Fig. 5F, the DPV responses of other miRNAs are similar to blank and markedly smaller than miR-21. All of experiment results demonstrate that the EC biosensor has a satisfactory stability, reproducibility and selectivity.
In order to study the practical application of the sensor, the standard addition experiments were implemented by adding the target miR-21 with known concentrations to $10\%$ blank human serum samples. Then, these samples were measured by our biosensor and the results were listed in Table S3, showing that the recoveries ranged from $95.66\%$ to $106.13\%$ with the RSD of $1.88\%{-2.68\%}$ . This satisfying accuracy indicates that the designed EC biosensor has a great potential for prac­ tical application.
## 4. Conclusion
In conclusion, a label-free and highly sensitive electrochemical biosensor was successfully developed for miR-21 detection based on the CHA-HCR dual amplification mode integrated with Cu-based metalorganic frameworks as the signal probes. To understanding the designed principle concisely, some conspicuous features of the biosensor are summarized as follows: (i) Benefiting from the enzyme-free dynamic DNA self-assembly (DDSA) amplification technique of CHA and HCR, the response signal of target achieves cascade amplification. (ii) More­ over, Cu-MOFs with high surface area serves as the excellent carriers to load HCR probes and acts as electrochemical labels, which avoids further labeling electrochemical signal probe and simplifies the fabri­ cation and detection steps. (iii) The sensitivity of this biosensor is significantly improved from two perspectives where the input amount of target is continuously magnified by CHA-HCR process and the output signals of Cu-MOFs is enhanced by combining Cu-MOFs with HCR. (iv) The excellent stability, reproducibility, selectivity and accuracy of the EC biosensor illustrates its practical potential for quantitative detection of other miRNAs in early clinical diagnosis. In a word, these advantages of the CHA-HCR-Cu-MOFs sensing strategy paves a new way to establish the label-free, enzyme-free and sensitive electrochemical biosensors for detection various target molecules.
